<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Real-world LLM agents must master strategic tool orchestration and user preference optimization through multi-turn interactions to assist users with complex planning tasks.
We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>onstrained <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization through <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ulti-turn <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>lanning <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a</span>nd <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>trategic <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>olutions), a benchmark that evaluates agents on realistic travel planning scenarios. We cast travel planning as a <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constrained preference optimization</span> problem, where agents must satisfy hard constraints while simultaneously optimizing soft user preferences. To support this, we build a realistic travel database covering transportation, accommodation and ticketing for 20 U.S. National Parks and a comprehensive tool ecosystem that mirrors commercial booking platforms.
Evaluating state-of-the-art models, we uncover two critical gaps: (<span id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span>) an <span id="abstract1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acceptable–optimal gap</span>, where agents reliably meet constraints but fail to optimize preferences, and <span id="abstract1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> a <span id="abstract1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">plan-coordination gap</span>, where performance collapses on multi-services (flight, hotel) coordination tasks, especially for open-source models.
By grounding reasoning and planning in a practical, user-facing domain, <span id="abstract1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> offers a benchmark that directly measures an agent’s ability to optimize user preferences in realistic tasks, bridging theoretical advances with real-world impact.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As Large Language Models (LLMs) move toward real-world deployment, their role is increasingly to assist users with everyday tasks that blend strict requirements with soft preferences. A natural way to formalize such interactions is through the lens of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constrained preference optimization</span>: agents must satisfy hard constraints (e.g., travel dates, budget limits, accessibility requirements) while simultaneously optimizing soft user preferences (e.g., cheapest options or wish-lists).
Within this framework, tool-mediated problem solving is the mechanism that enables agents to reason strategically and invoke external resources. By iteratively refining their plans, agents can build coherent solutions that align with user preferences.
This dual capacity for constraint satisfaction and preference optimization is therefore a core requirement for LLM agents to be genuinely useful in practice. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Data and code is available at: <a href="https://github.com/sunnytqin/compass.git" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/sunnytqin/compass.git</a></span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current benchmarks, however, fall short of evaluating these core capabilities. Most planning benchmarks reduce tasks to single-turn constraint satisfaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohli et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where finding any feasible solution is sufficient. Yet, preference optimization requires <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">qualitatively</span> different skills: rather than stopping at the first valid solution, agents must compare alternatives and select the one that best aligns with user preferences. Likewise, existing tool-use benchmarks focus narrowly on whether agents call the correct tool with valid parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, without testing how agents adapt to multi-turn interactions, where users naturally add new requirements and provide feedbacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laban et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Finally, most benchmarks rely on simplified mock databases. However, database realism directly shapes task difficulty—authentic evaluation requires rich, complex environments to meaningfully assess agent capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these gaps, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span>, a benchmark that instantiates constrained preference optimization in the setting of realistic travel planning. In <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span>, agents must satisfy hard constraints such as budget, occupancy, and scheduling while simultaneously optimizing soft preferences such as minimizing cost or maximizing wish-list amenities (Fig.<a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">B</span>). To support this evaluation, we construct a full environment consisting of <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> a modular LLM-based user simulator for controllable multi-turn interactions (Fig.<a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A, C</span>), <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> a realistic database of hotels, flights, and activity permits derived from commercial sources (Fig.<a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">E</span>), and <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(iii)</span> a tool ecosystem that mirrors booking platforms, enabling agents to search, filter, and combine information (Fig.<a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span>). Tasks are organized along two axes: <span id="S1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task types</span>, which define whether optimization is based on a single numerical metric or maximizing a checklist of desired features, and <span id="S1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task levels</span>, which range from hotel-only scenarios to full itineraries involving flights and permits. Together, these components provide a rigorous yet practical benchmark for assessing whether LLM agents can go beyond constraint satisfaction to deliver optimal, user-preference aligned solutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Through extensive evaluation of state-of-the-art models, we uncover two critical findings that reveal fundamental limitations in current agentic capabilities.
First, we observe a significant <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acceptable-optimal gap</span> (Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): while all models achieve high acceptable rates (constraint satisfaction), they show substantially lower optimal rates (preference optimization), indicating agents settle for feasible solutions rather than optimizing user preferences even when preference objectives are clearly specified.
Second, we identify a substantial <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">plan-coordination gap</span> (Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Validating LLM User Simulator Quality ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span>): performance degrades sharply once tasks require comprehensive planning across services (hotel, flight). At these levels, agents must align hotels, flights, and permits under temporal and budget constraints, and orchestrate multiple tool calls to maintain plan (itinerary) consistency. While closed-source models show gradual decline, open-source models struggle dramatically, highlighting the difficulty of coordination in realistic travel scenarios. Together, <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> serves as a rigorous benchmark for diagnosing the core challenges of constrained preference optimization and guiding the development of user-aligned AI agents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07043v1/benchmark_overview.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/262;" width="548" height="262" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
<span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">COMPASS<span id="S1.F1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> benchmark framework.</span></span>
The environment integrates three key components for realistic evaluation of agentic capabilities in travel planning.
<span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> A modular LLM-based user simulator enables controllable multi-turn interactions, progressive constraint revelation, and diverse user personas.
<span id="S1.F1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> We formalize travel planning as <span id="S1.F1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constrained preference optimization</span>, where agents must satisfy hard constraints (feasible solutions) while optimizing soft user objectives (e.g., minimizing cost, maximizing amenities).
<span id="S1.F1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C)</span> Agents interact with realistic travel databases <span id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(E)</span> using a comprehensive tool ecosystem <span id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D)</span>, requiring iterative planning and refinement across conversation turns to construct optimal itineraries.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07043v1/solve_optimal_gap.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/209;" width="494" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
<span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">COMPASS<span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> benchmark main results.</span></span>
<span id="S1.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acceptable rate</span> measures feasibility (satisfying all hard constraints). <span id="S1.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimal rate</span> measures preference optimization (achieving utility within the top 10% of feasible solutions). All models show a <math id="S1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 20\%</annotation></semantics></math> gap between high acceptable rates and low optimal rates, revealing that agents settle for feasible solutions rather than optimizing preferences. Encouragingly, open-source models like Qwen3-32B achieve non-trivial performance, demonstrating emerging agentic capabilities.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool-Use and Planning Benchmarks</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent benchmarks have evaluated language model agents across tool-use and planning capabilities, though typically in isolation. Tool-use benchmarks like Tau-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>)</cite> provide comprehensive API libraries but focus primarily on instruction-following in a online customer service setting. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al. (2025)</a></cite> includes a wider tool library but user multi-turn is based on predetermined conversation trajectories, while others  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> evaluate coding scenarios with oracle-like users. Planning benchmarks have emerged to assess constraint satisfaction and optimization: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a></cite> evaluates natural language planning tasks while eliminating tool-use complexity, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohli et al. (2024)</a></cite> presents flight booking scenarios in multiple-choice format without interactive tool integration. More comprehensive planning evaluation includes multi-day travel planning with hard constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, though without the multi-turn preference revelation that characterizes real user interactions. Our work is the first to combine tool-calling, planning, and preference optimization in a multi-turn setting—a combination essential for agents deployed in real-world scenarios where users progressively reveal constraints while agents must dynamically adapt their search and optimization strategies. Concurrent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al. (2025)</a></cite> also explores user preference optimization in a planning setting but with a stronger focus on preference elicitation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Turn Interactions and Task-Oriented Dialogue</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assessing agent capabilities in multi-turn settings is crucial for real-world deployment, as users naturally reveal requirements incrementally rather than specifying complete constraints upfront <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laban et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Traditional task-oriented dialogue systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Budzianowski et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rastogi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> provide foundational insights: multi-turn interactions occur because users reveal new requirements, request information, and adapt goals when constraints cannot be satisfied. However, these systems typically lack the tool integration and utility optimization challenges present in modern agent evaluation. Recent work on multi-turn agent evaluation includes simple tasks like city guessing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdulhai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and preference learning through turn-based rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but lacks the complexity of realistic planning scenarios. Drawing inspiration from task-oriented dialogue research, our user simulator design achieves diverse interaction dynamics through progressive constraint revelation, mimicking how real users gradually specify preferences while maintaining the authentic conversational patterns observed in human-to-human planning dialogues. We provide an extensive related work in App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related Work ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> Benchmark</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Travel Planning as Constrained Preference Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central challenge for real-world agents is to not only satisfy user requirements but also optimize for user satisfaction. To capture this duality, we formulate travel planning as a constrained preference optimization problem, where <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hard constraints</span> define the feasible set of solutions and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">soft preferences</span> rank those solutions by user preference. Hard constraints represent non-negotiable requirements—budget limits (“total cost under $3000”), occupancy needs (“6 people, max 2 per room”), or logistical requirements (“permit required for Extended Cave Tour”). Within this feasible set, user preference objectives capture user priorities such as minimizing cost, maximizing amenities. This separation between feasibility and desirability provides a principled way to evaluate whether agents can go beyond finding one valid solution to finding the best solution based on user requests.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07043v1/demo.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/212;" width="548" height="212"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Examples of task types and dynamic user simulator prompt.</span>
<span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> Two task types are defined based on the soft preference-optimization objective. Each task type includes hard constraints but differs in optimization objective (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
<span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> The dynamic LLM user simulator prompt (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 User Simulator Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) controls multi-turn conversation dynamics. The system prompt consists of static instructions (orange), fixed for each conversation, and dynamic fields (purple), which are updated at every turn based on the conversation state.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Task Design</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task Types</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real users have different ways of defining the “best” travel itinerary. To balance realism with clarity of evaluation, we design two complementary soft preference types that capture distinct optimization behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Metric Optimization Task.</span> The user specifies one numerical objective, and the agent optimizes along that single axis. For example: “find me the cheapest trip” (minimize cost) or “book the highest-rated hotel available” (maximize review score). Here, success means identifying the extreme value on a clearly defined metric.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Count Maximization Task.</span> The user provides a wish list of desirable attributes, such as “hotel with spa, pool, and in-room kitchen; flight with WiFi and direct routing.” These are not strict requirements—an itinerary that satisfies three out of five features is preferable to one that satisfies only one. The agent must therefore find the option that maximizes the total number of satisfied features, reflecting the realistic setting where users treat many preferences as flexible “nice-to-haves.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In both task types, each task <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">always</span> includes a set of hard constraints. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Travel Planning as Constrained Preference Optimization ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> shows examples of each task type. The optimization objectives are explicitly designed to be quantitatively measurable, ensuring that each task is well-specified from a benchmarking perspective. At the same time, when combined with hard constraints, these optimization objectives yield rich and realistic user behaviors. For instance, in a single-metric optimization task, a user may aim for the cheapest option (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">optimization objective</span>) while still requiring a hotel rated at least three stars (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hard constraint</span>). In a feature-count maximization task, a user may prefer business-class flights and luxury hotels (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">optimization objective</span>) but restrict total spending to under $2,000 (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hard constraint</span>). This combination balances benchmark clarity and comparability with realism and diversity in user preferences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">COMPASS<span id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> benchmark characteristics</span></span>.
<span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left:</span> Task distribution (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) across three complexity levels, and split between the two optimization types, and data scale covering geographic coverage, inventory size, and benchmark components.
<span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right:</span> Complete tool suite (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Database Construction &amp; Tools Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) organized by category, listing 18 APIs for navigation, search, validation, and utility functions.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:190.8pt;height:146.4pt;vertical-align:-70.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_thead">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Category</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Item</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Count</span></span></span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_4"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task Levels</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Level I (Hotel only)</span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">142</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Level II (Hotel, Flight)</span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">69</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Level III (Hotel, Flight, Permit)</span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">70</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Tasks</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">281</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task Types</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Single Metric Optimization</span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">154</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Feature Count Maximization</span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">127</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_5"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Database Scale</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">National Parks (Destinations)</span>
