</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task Levels</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each task type, we also vary the difficulty of tasks. We design three levels of increasing complexity, enabling systematic evaluation of agents’ planning and reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Level I (Hotel Only):</span> Agents must navigate room configurations and pricing structures under constraints such as budget, occupancy, and amenities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Level II (Hotel + Flight):</span> Agents plan complete trips with both accommodations and transportation. This requires temporal reasoning to align departure dates with hotel availability while distributing the budget across both services.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Level III (Hotel + Flight + Permits):</span> Agents incorporate reservations for guided tours or hiking experiences with limited availability. Permits introduce additional temporal constraints: permit availability can dictate travel dates, which in turn determine feasible hotel and flight options.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This progression serves three evaluation purposes:
(1) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Practical relevance</span> — comprehensive planning across services (hotel, flights) reflects real deployment scenarios, where an ideal agent should produce full itineraries that optimize user experience.
(2) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sequential reasoning</span> — higher levels require chaining constraints across domains, such as permit availability driving hotel selection, which then constrains flight booking.
(3) <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tool orchestration complexity</span> — agents must coordinate multiple APIs and handle compound constraints (e.g., “hotel within 3 hours’ drive of airport”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We design tasks using 36 diverse user templates (e.g., budget backpacker, family with children, wedding planner), each specifying a travel scenario with associated hard constraints and soft preferences. From these templates, we generate 240 total tasks across the three complexity levels by sampling parameters such as travel dates, destinations, and budget ranges (See Tab. <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Task Types ‣ 3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for task breakdown). We further vary task difficulty along multiple axes: number of constraints, temporal complexity (flexible vs. fixed dates), spatial complexity (single vs. multiple destinations), and required tool interaction depth. App. <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Task Statistics and Distribution ‣ Appendix D Additional Benchmark Details ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> provides a detailed breakdown of all tasks and concrete examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Database Construction &amp; Tools Design</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The complexity of the underlying data directly shapes the difficulty and realism of an optimization benchmark. Oversimplified databases fail to reflect real-world challenges, while overly complex ones reduce practical relevance. To balance these factors, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> uses real travel data obtained through RapidAPI,<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://rapidapi.com/DataCrawler/api/booking-com15" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://rapidapi.com/DataCrawler/api/booking-com15</a></span></span></span> constructing clean SQL databases from actual booking information. Our dataset covers 20 U.S. National Parks, each with 20 hotels offering diverse room types, prices, amenities, and policies. We also include flight data from four major airlines with varied routing options and booking classes. Permit data is mocked, but modeled after real high-demand permits (e.g., the Yosemite Half Dome hike <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/hdpermits.htm" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/hdpermits.htm</a></span></span></span>), ensuring scenarios remain authentic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> also provides a comprehensive tool suite that both mirrors commercial booking platforms and tests agents’ reasoning. Core search functions (search_hotel,search_flights) are complemented by utility tools (calculator, notebook) that support multi-step problem solving. Our search tools accept rich filters (e.g., price ranges, amenity requirements, routing preferences), providing agents the optionality to strategically conduct search. A full list of tools is shown in Tab. <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Task Types ‣ 3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(right)</span> and detailed in App. <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Available Tool Schema ‣ Appendix D Additional Benchmark Details ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>User Simulator Design</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use GPT-5 as the backbone of our user simulator. Unlike prior benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that rely on a single static prompt for the entire dialogue, our simulator employs <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dynamic prompting</span>—adjusting instructions both across different users and within each conversation turn. Compared to existing works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that use static user prompt designs, our simulator enables fine-grained control over user behavior. Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Travel Planning as Constrained Preference Optimization ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> shows a demonstration of the dynamic prompting template and App. <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 LLM User Simulator ‣ Appendix D Additional Benchmark Details ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> lists the verbatim prompt template. We control two <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">within-conversation dynamics</span>:
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Progressive constraint revelation</span>, where users disclose task information incrementally rather than upfront, and
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Trust and communication patterns</span>, where users vary in whether they accept recommendations immediately or demand explicit reasoning.
We also introduce two axes of <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">persona diversity</span>:
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Constraint-checking reliability</span>, where users differ in how consistently they detect and correct agent mistakes, and
<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Conversation style</span>, where personas range from concise and pragmatic to repetitive, emotional, or insistent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At each dialogue turn, the dynamic sections of the user prompt are updated to reflect the current conversation state—what task information has been revealed, what remains to be revealed, the user’s feedback on the agent’s last response, and whether the user should trigger questioning behaviors (e.g., asking the agent to double-check a recommendation). Static sections, by contrast, encode user persona and task-level context. Together, this dynamic prompting framework produces diverse yet controlled conversations, allowing systematic evaluation of how agents adapt to different users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Evaluation Metric Design</h3>

<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ground Truth Generation</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate agents reliably, we construct ground truth solutions for every task through exhaustive search. We begin by enumerating all candidate hotels, flights (Levels II &amp; III), and permits (Level III), applying single-domain filters (e.g., direct flights, 4-star hotels). We then generate all possible cross-domain combinations and eliminate any that violate hard constraints such as budget limits or airport–hotel driving times. The remaining set defines the complete feasible space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For both task types (Sec <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), we compute an exact <span id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">utility score</span>. In single-metric optimization tasks, the score is the numerical value of the target metric (e.g., total price, average review score). In feature-count maximization tasks, the score is the number of desired features satisfied. This exhaustive approach guarantees correctness, since every feasible solution is enumerated and scored. We further validate ground truth quality by discarding tasks with too few feasible solutions or with too many tied optima (<math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>20% of the feasible set), ensuring each task poses a meaningful optimization challenge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From this solution library, we evaluate agents along two dimensions.
For <span id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constraint satisfaction</span>, we check whether at least one agent-recommended itinerary belongs to the feasible set, reporting the acceptable rate.
For <span id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">preference optimization</span>, we assess how close the agent’s best recommendation comes to the top-performing feasible solutions, reporting the optimality rate. We provide formal definition below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acceptable Rate</span>: Fraction of tasks agent’s recommendation satisfies user’s hard constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimality Rate</span>: Fraction of tasks where agent’s recommendation falls within the top <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th percentile.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human evaluation of LLM user simulator quality.</span>
<span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left:</span> Scoring rubrics used by annotators for <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clarity</span> and <span id="S4.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual appropriateness</span>.
<span id="S4.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right:</span> Human evaluation results on 198 user responses. Rubric scores are reported as mean <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation, as well as median, and quantile cut-offs. Error rates are reported as percentages of responses exhibiting each error type. </figcaption>
