<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> on a range of frontier closed-source models, including both reasoning-enabled and standard versions of GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025)</a></cite>, Claude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2025)</a></cite>, and Gemini<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as well as the frontier open-source model Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. All models support native tool calling, and we pass the tool schema directly to the model interface. To standardize outputs, agents are required to return a JSON object listing all “package_ids”, which uniquely identifies a booking configuration, in the required fields (“flights”/“hotel”/“permit”), or leave them empty if not applicable. We evaluate each agent on the final itinerary it produces. Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports aggregate performance. App. <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Experiment Details ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> provides inference details for each model, App. <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Full Benchmark Result ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> reports a detailed breakdown table of model performances, and App. <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Benchmark Stability ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> confirms benchmark stability with small error bars across repeated runs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We find a significant <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gap between constrained-preference satisfaction and preference optimization</span>. All models achieve high acceptable rates, showing they can interpret constraints and return feasible solutions. However, their 5% and 10% optimality rates are substantially lower, revealing that agents often settle for feasible but suboptimal solutions even when objectives are simple and explicitly stated. Reasoning models outperform non-reasoning counterparts, and open-source Qwen3-32B (reasoning enabled) reaches close to 20% optimality rate—comparable to some closed-source systems such as GPT-4o. This suggests that strong agentic capabilities are emerging in open-source models. Nonetheless, GPT-5 shows <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">superior performance across both dimensions</span>, outperforming all other models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Validating LLM User Simulator Quality</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure our LLM user simulator produces realistic and reliable interactions, we randomly sample 45 full conversations spanning diverse personas, task types, and agent interactions. From these, we extract <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">198</span> individual user responses for human evaluation by an independent expert annotation service, covering the full range of scenarios in the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Annotators assess each response along two dimensions. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Rubric-based quality assessment:</span> responses are scored on two axes. The <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clarity</span> axis measures whether user messages are expressed in a way that avoids misleading the agent or causing task failure; a score below 3 (on a 1–5 scale) indicates that the simulator may have provided confusing or incorrect task information. The <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual appropriateness</span> axis evaluates whether responses flow naturally within a travel-planning dialogue. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Detailed error detection:</span> since behaviors such as constraint revelation and trust patterns are explicitly controlled (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 User Simulator Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), annotators tag whether the simulator follows its prompts correctly without hallucination or deviation. The scoring rubric appears in Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(left)</span>, with full annotation instructions and error definitions in App. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Human Evaluation ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Results (Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">right</span>) show high quality scores (median clarity: 4; contextual appropriateness: 4 on a 1–5 scale) and very low error rates—4% for constraint revelation and 0.5% for feedback accuracy. These results confirm that the simulator reliably follows its script while producing clear, natural responses, validating it as a realistic tool for benchmarking agent capabilities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07043v1/main_analysis.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/303;" width="548" height="303"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S4.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance breakdown across benchmark dimensions.</span>
<span id="S4.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> Performance degrades with increasing plan coordination complexity (Levels II–III), with open-source models showing especially steep declines (<span id="S4.F4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">green</span>).
<span id="S4.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> Constraint satisfaction rates drop as the number of hard constraints increases, with only the strongest models handling 8+ constraints reliably.
<span id="S4.F4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C)</span> Preference optimization weakens as search complexity grows (more searches required to reach the ground-truth optimum).
<span id="S4.F4.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D)</span> Conversation efficiency analysis: how fast agent achieves solutions with the fewest post-information revelation turns.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>In-Depth Analysis</h2>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07043v1/case_study.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/338;" width="548" height="338" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
<span id="S5.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case study of tool calls and reasoning traces.</span>
<span id="S5.F5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Top:</span> Prompt given to models for a Level II task, with explicit reasoning requested.
<span id="S5.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bottom:</span> GPT-5 (<span id="S5.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">left</span>) demonstrates strategic planning by avoiding weekends, systematically exploring date ranges, and using optional parameters (e.g., price filters) to narrow searches. Claude-Sonnet-4 (<span id="S5.F5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">right</span>) applies optional parameters but searches only two arbitrary dates without justification. It also makes a temporal coordination error by misaligning hotel and flight dates.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Why Do Agents Struggle in Travel Planning?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central question is why agents sometimes fail to identify feasible solutions or converge to suboptimal outcomes. To investigate, we analyze performance across <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> tasks and identify statistical patterns linked to failure, breaking down results by three fundamental task characteristics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Plan-Coordination Complexity</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first analyze performance across the three task levels (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Task Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) to test how increasing planning demands affect agents. Higher levels expand the optimization scope (from single hotels to multi-service itineraries), enlarge the combinatorial solution space, and introduce temporal dependencies that require sequential reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Validating LLM User Simulator Quality ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> shows a sharp decline in constrained-preference satisfaction as task complexity increases (similar optimality trends in App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Additional Analysis Results ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.3 Additional Analysis Results ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). While most models handle Level I reliably, performance drops substantially on Level II’s cross-service coordination and collapses on Level III’s temporal reasoning. Notably, open-source models perform competitively with frontier closed-source systems such as GPT-4o and Gemini-2.5-Flash on Level I tasks, but their performance degrades far more steeply on Levels II–III. This divergence underscores a critical weakness: while open-source models can handle single-service(task) optimization, they struggle disproportionately with cross-services coordination and sequential reasoning. This highlights a key limitation: current agents struggle to propagate constraints and plan coordination, with the challenge most acute for open-source models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Constraint Complexity</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Constraint complexity measures how well agents handle tasks with increasing numbers of hard requirements. Each task includes at least five fundamental constraints (e.g., location, dates, occupancy, budget), with additional non-negotiable requirements such as amenities or policies further restricting feasibility. Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Validating LLM User Simulator Quality ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> shows performance degrades as constraint count rises. GPT-5 and Claude maintain high constrained-preference satisfaction even with 8+ constraints, whereas most other models drop sharply beyond 7. This indicates that despite users explicitly stating requirements and correcting violations, many agents struggle to reliably track and enforce multiple constraints—highlighting that robust constraint management remains limited to the most advanced models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimization Complexity</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond satisfying hard constraints, agents should aim for best option. We capture this through <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">search complexity</span>, defined as the number of distinct searches required to guarantee finding the optimum. For example, a request for the cheapest weekend in August at Yosemite requires searching all four weekends (search complexity = 4). Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Validating LLM User Simulator Quality ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C)</span> reports optimality rates only on tasks where the agent produced a feasible solution—that is, among valid recommendations, we measure how often they fall within the top 5th percentile of all feasible options. When search complexity is minimal (1 search, with fully specified dates and location), agents achieve high optimality. As complexity increases—medium (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>5 searches) to hard (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>5 searches)—optimal rates decline across all models. Even GPT-5 drops from over 80% on easy tasks to around 60% on hard tasks. This demonstrates that while agents can reliably find feasible solutions, they often fail to explore the solution space deeply enough to identify the truly optimal choice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Conversation Efficiency</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond final performance (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), efficiency is critical for user-facing agents, as long, drawn-out interactions often cause frustration. We measure efficiency by leveraging our controlled user simulator (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 User Simulator Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). For each conversation, we record the turn <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> when the user has disclosed all hard constraints and preference objectives, and compute the extra turns <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> the agent requires to produce its final recommendation. Extra turns typically arise when agents either fail to provide a recommendation or recommend options that violate constraints—in both cases, the user continues prompting the agent to try again until a valid solution is provided or max-turn (10) runs out.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Validating LLM User Simulator Quality ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D)</span> shows that GPT-5 is superior not only in task success but also in conversational economy, consistently requiring the fewest post-revelation turns. In App. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Additional Conversation Statistics ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, we confirm that the observed difference is not caused by variance in user simulator behavior. Importantly, efficiency varies even among models with similar success rates: for instance, Gemini-2.5-Flash takes substantially more turns than GPT-4o despite comparable final outcomes. This highlights that raw success metrics alone are insufficient—how efficiently an agent reaches its solution is just as important as whether it succeeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Case Study: Smart Strategies for Travel Planning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our ground truth relies on exhaustive search (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Evaluation Metric Design ‣ 3 COMPASS Benchmark ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>), real travel planning requires reasoning, strategy, and efficient tool use. To examine whether agents display such behaviors, we analyze their tool usage and reasoning on a representative Level II task, asking them to explain their thought process (Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 In-Depth Analysis ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top</span>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Strategic vs. Naive Planning</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5 exhibits strategic behavior (Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 In-Depth Analysis ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bottom left</span>). Rather than searching blindly, it checks the calendar, targets weekday departures (anticipating midweek price drops), and samples across August to ensure coverage. This heuristic aligns with our data—midweek trips are indeed cheaper on average—but whether it is “smart” depends on context, as models may apply incorrect heuristics elsewhere. A more capable agent would either (i) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verify</span> such assumptions or (ii) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">articulate</span> them to the user for confirmation. GPT-5 does the latter, making its reasoning transparent and allowing user-guided refinement. Nevertheless, it also adds unjustified parameters—e.g., enforcing <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">free_cancellation=True</span>—which may exclude valid cheaper options, illustrating the trade-off between strategic search and fidelity to user intent. Claude Sonnet-4 (Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 In-Depth Analysis ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bottom right</span>) behaves more naively: it searches only two dates without justification and commits a major coordination error by misaligning hotel and flight dates. Its reasoning traces are generic (“<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gives exactly what you requested</span>”), concealing such mistakes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These results show that intelligent travel planning requires more than constraint satisfaction or exhaustive search; it depends on heuristics and reasoning grounded in tool use. We expect future agents to employ richer strategies, such as maintaining a running “best” option and progressively filtering around it, balancing efficiency with optimality. Overall, our case studies demonstrate that COMPASS evaluates reasoning-driven planning behaviors beyond mere tool execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span>, a benchmark for evaluating LLM agents on constrained-preference optimization in realistic travel planning. The environment integrates three core components—a controllable user simulator, structured databases of hotels, flights, and permits, and a tool ecosystem mirroring commercial booking platforms. Tasks are organized along two axes, optimization type and planning level, to jointly assess constraint satisfaction and preference optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span>, we evaluated frontier models and revealed two key limitations: an <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">acceptable–optimal gap</span>, where agents stop at feasible but suboptimal solutions, and a <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plan-coordination gap</span>, where performance collapses when aligning multiple services under temporal and budget constraints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark scope and practical relevance.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Travel planning is both a realistic use case and a rich testbed for evaluating agentic reasoning. Our benchmark currently focuses on flights, hotels, and permits, but real itineraries often involve additional elements—car rentals, restaurants, or multi-destination trips—where agents must balance commute times and sequence visits efficiently. Extending <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> to web-based environments, where agents navigate unstructured webpages and inconsistent interfaces, would further enhance task realism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">User interactions and evaluation realism.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The user simulator enables controllable multi-turn dynamics, but real users exhibit greater variability—changing goals mid-dialogue, providing underspecified inputs, or asking for clarifications. Expanding these behaviors would help evaluate agent robustness in more naturalistic settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning and agentic capabilities.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While ground-truth solutions rely on exhaustive search, effective planning requires reasoning and heuristic strategies. As seen in our case study (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Case Study: Smart Strategies for Travel Planning ‣ 5 In-Depth Analysis ‣ COMPASS: A Multi-Turn Benchmark for Tool-Mediated Planning &amp; Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>), capable agents use simplifications such as strategic date selection or parameterized searches to approximate optimality. Future work should explore structured agent workflows—planning–execution pipelines, preference-tracking memory, or multi-agent collaboration—to address limitations in native tool use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">COMPASS</span> establishes constrained-preference optimization as a unified framework for studying agent reasoning and exposes concrete challenges that must be solved for LLMs to act as trustworthy, user-aligned assistants in the real world.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
